  "live_records": {
    "q-53562": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53562",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=3,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}-0] \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
