    "q-124191": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124191",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=2,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
