    "q-352345": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352345",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{n(n+1)}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=3,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & =9[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =9[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =9[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =9[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=9[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }9[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =9[\\frac{1}{3}-0] \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
