    "q-83582": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83582",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*",
      "worked_solution": "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3}=\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+3)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+1}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+1}-\\frac{1}{n+3})=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+1}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{10})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\frac{1}{6}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{10})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{11})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{6},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+2}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+3}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+2}-\\frac{1}{N+3}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n+3} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+2}-\\frac{1}{N+3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }⁡[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{N+2}-\\frac{1}{N+3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{6}-0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{11}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
