      "dom_id": "step-q124521",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4})=6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+4}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =6[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{1} & =6[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{2} & =6[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+4}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+2)(n+4)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =6{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}-\\frac{1}{N+4}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0-0] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
