    "q-352380": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352380",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Evaluate the series\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}.$\nDo not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*\n\n${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We can factor the denominator and use partial fractions to show that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-6n+8}=\\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2})=2{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-4}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =2[(1-\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =2[(1-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{6})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $1,\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N-3}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N-2}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}-6n+8} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{N-3}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =2(1+\\frac{1}{2}-0-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
